<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <RootNamespace>Vixen.Samples.HelloUi</RootNamespace>
        <AssemblyName>HelloUi</AssemblyName>
        <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
        <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
        <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
            ⚠ **No Vixen.App and no Vixen.Engine, and that absence is the sample.**
            docs/plan/02 § Samples calls this one "Vixen.Ui only, no engine — proves the UI/Engine
            boundary", and a reference to the host would quietly undo it: Vixen.App references
            Vixen.Engine, so taking the convenient way in would drag a scene, an ECS world and a game
            loop into an application that is a user interface. Everything Vixen.App would have done —
            the window, the device, the frame loop — is Program.cs, and it is ninety lines.

            `CheckArchitecture` asserts the absence, so this cannot be undone by accident.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Ui.Controls.Advanced\Vixen.Ui.Controls.Advanced.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Ui.Renderer\Vixen.Ui.Renderer.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Graphics.RenderGraph\Vixen.Graphics.RenderGraph.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Platform\Vixen.Platform.Desktop\Vixen.Platform.Desktop.csproj" />

        <!--
            The platform-to-document join. This sample carried its own copy of it and said, in a
            comment, that a Vixen.Platform.Ui was where it belonged once the editor became the
            second consumer. It has.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Platform\Vixen.Platform.Ui\Vixen.Platform.Ui.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Platform\Vixen.Graphics.Vulkan\Vixen.Graphics.Vulkan.csproj" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
            SPIR-V, committed, for the reason Samples/01 gives and one more: `UiRenderer`'s own
            remarks say the modules are "supplied rather than compiled here" because turning shader
            source into modules belongs to Raven — which already carries Ui/Msdf.rvn and
            Ui/RoundedRect.rvn for exactly this. Until that path is wired, a caller hands over
            whatever it has. These four are the same GLSL the golden-image fixture drives the
            renderer with, so the sample and the reference pictures cannot disagree about what the
            shaders do.

            Regenerating: `glslc Shaders/ui.vert -o Shaders/ui.vert.spv`, and likewise for the three
            fragment stages.
        -->
        <EmbeddedResource Include="Shaders\*.spv" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
